+# ttlcache: summary
+
+## What was wrong
+
+- **Loader ran twice for one key.** `get` had no single-flight: two threads that missed at the same time both
+  called the loader. Fixed by an in-flight record per key; other callers wait for it.
+- **Expired value served.** The hit check read the value and its timestamp without a lock, and used `<=`, so an
+  entry exactly `ttl` old was still served. Now the check and the read happen under one lock, and an entry is
+  valid only while `age < ttl`.
+- **Tests all erred.** `TTLCache` did not accept the `clock=` argument the tests pass.
+
+## What changed
+
+- `ttlcache/cache.py` (rewritten): one lock with a condition variable; the loader runs outside the lock.
+  - Single load, shared success and failure, retry after failure, `None`/falsy values stored as values.
+  - `get(key, timeout=None)`: waiting uses the condition; `timeout=0` raises `TimeoutError` at once; a timed-out
+    waiter leaves the load alone.
+  - Recursion (same thread owns the key) raises `RuntimeError`.
+  - Cross-thread cycles raise `DeadlockError` (subclass of `RuntimeError`), found by walking the
+    "thread waits on load, load owned by thread" graph under the lock. Timed waits count as waits.
+  - `max_size`: LRU eviction of entries whose key has no load in flight; a just-stored entry can be evicted.
+  - `stale_grace`: a failed load answers its owner and waiters with the stale entry, decided when the failure
+    is recorded. `BaseException`s other than `Exception` never produce a stale value.
+  - `invalidate`, `invalidate_all` (returns the count), `len`, `stats(extended=False)`.
+  - Argument validation raises `ValueError` as the contract says; a negative `timeout` is not counted.
+- `ttlcache/stats.py`: the four base counters plus `evictions`, `stale_served`, `timeouts`, `deadlocks`.
+- `ttlcache/__init__.py`: also exports `DeadlockError`.
+- `tests/test_contract.py` (new, 44 tests in the whole suite): one test group per guarantee. Existing tests are
+  unchanged.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `python -m unittest` (Python 3.14.3): **44 tests, OK** (the 9 original tests plus 35 new ones).
+- Repeated the full suite 30 times with a helper script (`python -I /tmp/ttl_repeat.py 30 <workspace>`):
+  **0 failed runs of 30**.
+- `git status` shows only `ttlcache/`, `tests/test_contract.py` and this file changed; `tests/test_cache.py`
+  has no diff.
+- Two bugs in my own first draft were caught by the new tests and fixed: a key was still marked in flight
+  while its value was evicted (so the just-stored key was protected), and some test call sites were wrong.
+
+## Not verified / open points
+
+- Not run under a free-threaded build, other Python versions, or sustained load; the concurrency tests use
+  events, not timing, but the 30 repeats are the only evidence of stability.
+- Deadlock detection is tested for a two-thread cycle and for in-thread chains, not for a three-thread cycle,
+  although the walk in `_completes_cycle` is written for any length.
+- The injected `clock` is called while the cache lock is held, so a clock that calls back into the cache would
+  deadlock. The contract does not forbid this, so it is a limitation to be aware of.
+- Judgment calls where the contract is not explicit: a `BaseException` (e.g. `KeyboardInterrupt`) from a loader
+  is never answered with a stale value; if the clock itself raises while a failure is recorded, that clock
+  exception propagates instead of the loader's.
+- Argument checks: `ttl`, `stale_grace` and `timeout` must be `numbers.Real` (not `bool`); `max_size` must be a
+  plain `int` (not `bool`). Other numeric types, such as `Decimal` or numpy integers, are rejected and I did not
+  test them.
+- `README.md` was not changed (it is outside the files I was allowed to change). Its examples still match the API.
+- CI config is not in the repository, so I could not check the CI setup itself; I ran the command you gave
+  (`python -m unittest`).
